Behavioral patterns
The following behavioral patterns can be used to define the communication between classes
and objects:
Chain of Responsibility: A pattern for handling a request between a collection of
objects
Command: A pattern used to represent a request
Interpreter: A pattern for defining syntax or language for instructions in a
program
Iterator: A pattern for traversing a collection of items without detailed
knowledge of the elements in a collection
Mediator: A pattern for simplifying communication between classes
Memento: A pattern for capturing and storing the state of an object
Observer: A pattern for allowing objects to be notified of changes to another
object's state
State: A pattern for altering an object's behavior when its state changes
Strategy: A pattern for implementing a collection of algorithms where a specific
algorithm can be applied at runtime
Template Method: A pattern for defining the steps of an algorithm while leaving
the implementation details in a subclass
Visitor: A pattern promoting loose coupling between data and functionality,

