The above method breaks this principle because adding a new command would alter the
behavior of the code. The idea of the principle is that it is closed to modification that would
alter its behavior and instead is open to extend the class to support additional behavior.
This is what is accomplished by having the abstract InventoryCommand and derived
classes (for example, QuitCommand, HelpCommand, and AddInventoryCommand). A
compelling reason for this, especially when combined with the other principles, is that it
leads to succinct code that is easier to maintain and understand.
